// Chemistry, Advanced Business Management, Spanish 2, Math 3 readings.
export default {
  // ---------------- Chemistry ----------------
  'chem/u1': {
    why: 'Chemistry is a measuring science. Before you can calculate anything, you need to know what matter is doing and how to handle units and precision. **Dimensional analysis** is the skill you will use in every unit after this one.',
    guide: ['How do you tell a physical change from a chemical change?', 'How do units cancel in dimensional analysis?', 'Why do significant figures matter?'],
    s: [
      ['Matter and change', '**Matter** is anything with mass that takes up space. It exists as **solids** (fixed shape), **liquids** (fixed volume, takes the container\'s shape) and **gases** (fill any container). A **physical change** alters form but not identity - melting ice is still water. A **chemical change** creates a **new substance**, like iron rusting or wood burning.', 'Clues to a chemical change: a new color, gas bubbles, a precipitate (a solid forming in a liquid), light, or a temperature change that wasn\'t caused by heating.'],
      ['Units and dimensional analysis', 'Scientists use **SI units**: meters, kilograms, seconds, moles, kelvin. **Dimensional analysis** converts units by multiplying by **conversion factors** - fractions equal to 1, like (1000 g / 1 kg). You arrange each factor so the unit you don\'t want **cancels**.', 'If your units cancel down to the unit you want, your setup is correct even before you touch a calculator. That\'s the safety check that catches most mistakes. **Density** is a common example: density = mass ÷ volume, measured in g/mL or g/cm³.'],
      ['Precision and sig figs', 'Every measurement has limited precision, and **significant figures** show how precise it is. Rules: nonzero digits always count; zeros between nonzero digits count; **leading zeros never count** (0.0045 has 2); **trailing zeros count only if there is a decimal point** (1200 has 2, 1200. has 4).', 'When multiplying or dividing, round to the **fewest sig figs** in the problem. When adding or subtracting, round to the **fewest decimal places**.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'new substance': 'The one test that matters: is it still the same substance? If yes, physical. If no, chemical.', 'cancels': 'Write units on every number. If a unit is left over that you didn\'t want, flip that conversion factor.', 'leading zeros never count': 'Leading zeros are only placeholders - they show where the decimal is, not how precise you measured.' },
    recall: [['Is dissolving sugar in water physical or chemical? Why?', 'Physical - it\'s still sugar and water; no new substance forms.'], ['How do you know a dimensional analysis setup is right?', 'All units cancel except the one you want.'], ['How many sig figs in 0.00450 and in 1200?', '0.00450 has 3; 1200 has 2.']],
    recap: ['Physical change = same substance; chemical change = new substance.', 'Dimensional analysis: multiply by conversion factors so units cancel.', 'Density = mass ÷ volume.', 'Sig figs: ×/÷ fewest sig figs; +/− fewest decimal places.'],
  },
  'chem/u2': {
    why: 'Everything in chemistry - bonding, reactions, properties - comes from **electrons**. Understanding the atom and the **periodic trends** lets you predict how elements behave without memorizing each one.',
    guide: ['What are atoms made of, and what makes isotopes different?', 'How are electrons arranged?', 'Why do atomic radius and electronegativity change across the table?'],
    s: [
      ['Inside the atom', 'An atom has a tiny **nucleus** of **protons** (+) and **neutrons** (neutral), surrounded by **electrons** (−). The **atomic number** is the number of protons and defines the element. The **mass number** = protons + neutrons.', '**Isotopes** are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons, like carbon-12 and carbon-14. The **atomic mass** on the periodic table is a weighted average of all the isotopes.'],
      ['Electron configuration', 'Electrons fill **energy levels** and **sublevels** (s, p, d, f) from lowest energy up. An s sublevel holds 2 electrons, p holds 6, d holds 10. Oxygen (8 electrons) is **1s² 2s² 2p⁴**.', 'The outermost electrons are the **valence electrons**. They decide how an atom bonds. Elements in the same **group** (column) have the same number of valence electrons, which is why they behave alike.'],
      ['Periodic trends', '**Atomic radius** gets **larger down a group** (more energy levels) and **smaller across a period** (more protons pull the same level closer). **Ionization energy** (energy to remove an electron) and **electronegativity** (pull on shared electrons) do the opposite: they increase **up and to the right**. **Fluorine** is the most electronegative element.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'atomic number': 'Change the protons and you change the element. Change the neutrons and you get an isotope. Change the electrons and you get an ion.', 'valence electrons': 'Valence electrons are the "hands" an atom uses to bond. Almost everything later depends on them.', 'smaller across a period': 'Counterintuitive: atoms get SMALLER going right even though they have more electrons, because more protons pull harder.' },
    recall: [['An atom has 17 protons and 18 neutrons. What is its mass number and element?', 'Mass number 35; chlorine (17 protons).'], ['Why do elements in a group behave alike?', 'Same number of valence electrons.'], ['Which is bigger, Na or Cl, and why?', 'Na - same period, but Cl has more protons pulling electrons closer.']],
    recap: ['Protons = identity; neutrons vary in isotopes; electrons in levels.', 'Configuration fills s (2), p (6), d (10) in energy order.', 'Valence electrons drive bonding; same group = same valence.', 'Radius: bigger down, smaller right. Electronegativity/IE: bigger up and right.'],
  },
  'chem/u3': {
    why: 'Bonding explains why salt dissolves, why water bends, and why oil and water don\'t mix. Naming compounds correctly also depends on knowing the bond type first.',
    guide: ['What is the difference between ionic and covalent bonds?', 'How does VSEPR predict molecular shape?', 'How do naming rules differ for ionic and covalent compounds?'],
    s: [
      ['Ionic vs covalent', 'In an **ionic bond**, a metal **transfers** electrons to a nonmetal, forming oppositely charged ions that attract (NaCl). In a **covalent bond**, nonmetals **share** electrons (H₂O, CO₂). The bigger the **electronegativity difference**, the more ionic the bond.', 'Covalent bonds can be **nonpolar** (equal sharing, like H₂) or **polar** (unequal sharing, like the O-H bonds in water, where oxygen pulls harder).'],
      ['Shapes with VSEPR', '**VSEPR** (valence shell electron pair repulsion) says electron pairs around a central atom push as far apart as possible. Four bonding pairs make a **tetrahedral** shape (CH₄). Three bonds plus one lone pair make a **trigonal pyramidal** shape (NH₃). Two bonds plus two lone pairs make a **bent** shape (H₂O).', 'Shape matters because it decides **polarity**: water is bent, so its polar bonds don\'t cancel and the molecule is polar. CO₂ is linear, so its polar bonds cancel and the molecule is nonpolar.'],
      ['Naming', '**Ionic**: name the metal, then the nonmetal with -ide - no prefixes (CaCl₂ is calcium chloride). **Covalent**: use prefixes - mono, di, tri, tetra, penta, hexa - but skip "mono" on the first element (N₂O₄ is dinitrogen tetroxide; CO is carbon monoxide).'],
    ],
    notes: { 'transfers': 'Metal + nonmetal = ionic (transfer). Nonmetal + nonmetal = covalent (share). Check for a metal first.', 'lone pair': 'Lone pairs take up space and squeeze the bonds together - that\'s why water is bent, not straight.', 'no prefixes': 'Prefixes are ONLY for covalent compounds. "Calcium dichloride" is a classic wrong answer.' },
    recall: [['Is KBr ionic or covalent?', 'Ionic - potassium is a metal, bromine is a nonmetal.'], ['Why is water polar but CO₂ nonpolar?', 'Water is bent so dipoles don\'t cancel; CO₂ is linear so they cancel.'], ['Name P₂O₅.', 'Diphosphorus pentoxide.']],
    recap: ['Ionic = transfer (metal + nonmetal); covalent = share.', 'VSEPR: 4 pairs tetrahedral; 3 + 1 lone pyramidal; 2 + 2 lone bent.', 'Shape decides polarity.', 'Ionic names: no prefixes. Covalent: prefixes, no mono on the first element.'],
  },
  'chem/u4': {
    why: 'Atoms are too small to count, so chemists count them in **moles**. The mole is the bridge between the grams you can weigh and the particles that actually react.',
    guide: ['What is a mole and why do we need it?', 'How do you move between grams, moles, and particles?', 'How do you find an empirical formula?'],
    s: [
      ['The mole', 'A **mole** is 6.022 × 10²³ particles - **Avogadro\'s number**. It\'s like a "dozen," just enormous. One mole of any substance has a mass in grams equal to its **molar mass**, which you get by adding the atomic masses from the periodic table. Water: 2(1.008) + 16.00 = **18.02 g/mol**.', 'Think of moles as the hub of a map. Grams connect to moles through molar mass. Particles connect to moles through Avogadro\'s number. You never go from grams to particles directly.'],
      ['Conversions', 'Grams → moles: divide by molar mass. Moles → grams: multiply by molar mass. Moles → particles: multiply by 6.022 × 10²³. Set each step up with dimensional analysis so units cancel.', '**Percent composition** tells what fraction of a compound\'s mass comes from each element: (mass of element ÷ molar mass of compound) × 100.'],
      ['Empirical formulas', 'The **empirical formula** is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. To find it from percentages: assume a 100 g sample (so % becomes grams), convert each to moles, **divide by the smallest**, and round to whole numbers. The **molecular formula** is a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula (glucose C₆H₁₂O₆ has the empirical formula CH₂O).'],
    ],
    notes: { 'Avogadro\'s number': 'You don\'t need to picture 6.022 × 10²³. Just treat "mole" like a unit, the way you treat "dozen".', 'divide by the smallest': 'If you get something like 1.5, multiply everything by 2 to reach whole numbers.' },
    recall: [['What is the molar mass of CO₂?', '12.01 + 2(16.00) = 44.01 g/mol.'], ['How many moles are in 36 g of water?', '36 ÷ 18.02 ≈ 2.0 mol.'], ['What are the steps to an empirical formula from percentages?', 'Assume 100 g → grams to moles → divide by smallest → whole-number ratio.']],
    recap: ['1 mol = 6.022 × 10²³ particles; molar mass = g/mol from the table.', 'Grams ↔ moles via molar mass; moles ↔ particles via Avogadro.', 'Percent composition = part ÷ whole × 100.', 'Empirical formula = simplest ratio; molecular = multiple of it.'],
  },
  'chem/u5': {
    why: 'Reactions are what chemistry is about. **Stoichiometry** lets you predict how much product you\'ll make - the same math used to scale recipes, medicines and industrial processes.',
    guide: ['Why must equations be balanced?', 'What are the main reaction types?', 'How do you use mole ratios to predict amounts?'],
    s: [
      ['Balancing equations', 'Atoms aren\'t created or destroyed (**conservation of mass**), so every equation must have the same number of each atom on both sides. You balance by changing **coefficients** (the big numbers in front), never subscripts. 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O has 4 H and 2 O on each side.', 'Main types: **synthesis** (A + B → AB), **decomposition** (AB → A + B), **single replacement** (A + BC → AC + B), **double replacement** (AB + CD → AD + CB), and **combustion** (fuel + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O).'],
      ['Stoichiometry', 'The coefficients are a **mole ratio**. In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 3 moles of H₂ make 2 moles of NH₃. Every stoichiometry problem is the same three moves: **grams → moles → mole ratio → grams**.', 'The **limiting reactant** is the one that runs out first, so it decides how much product forms. Work out the product each reactant could make - the smaller answer wins. **Percent yield** = (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'never subscripts': 'Changing a subscript changes the substance itself - H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide, not water.', 'mole ratio': 'The ratio only works in MOLES, never grams. Always convert to moles first.' },
    recall: [['Balance: _CH₄ + _O₂ → _CO₂ + _H₂O', '1, 2, 1, 2.'], ['What are the three moves in every stoichiometry problem?', 'Grams → moles, use the mole ratio, moles → grams.'], ['How do you find the limiting reactant?', 'Calculate the product from each reactant; the one giving less product is limiting.']],
    recap: ['Balance with coefficients only (conservation of mass).', 'Five reaction types: synthesis, decomposition, single, double, combustion.', 'Coefficients = mole ratio; grams → moles → ratio → grams.', 'Limiting reactant decides product; percent yield = actual ÷ theoretical.'],
  },
  'chem/u6': {
    why: 'Gases explain tire pressure, weather balloons and why a sealed bag puffs up on a plane. The gas laws are one idea with a few special cases.',
    guide: ['How does kinetic molecular theory explain gas behavior?', 'How do pressure, volume and temperature relate?', 'When do you use PV = nRT?'],
    s: [
      ['Kinetic molecular theory', '**Kinetic molecular theory** says gas particles move fast and randomly, are far apart, and collide elastically. **Temperature** measures their average kinetic energy. **Pressure** comes from particles hitting the container walls.', 'That explains everything: heat a gas and the particles move faster, hitting the walls harder and more often, so pressure rises - or the gas expands if it can.'],
      ['The gas laws', '**Boyle\'s law**: at constant temperature, pressure and volume are inversely related (P₁V₁ = P₂V₂). **Charles\'s law**: at constant pressure, volume and temperature are directly related (V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂). **Gay-Lussac\'s law**: at constant volume, pressure and temperature are directly related. The **combined gas law** puts them together.', 'Temperature must always be in **kelvin** (K = °C + 273). At zero kelvin particles would stop moving, so kelvin is the only scale where "double the temperature" really means double the energy.', 'The **ideal gas law**, **PV = nRT**, adds moles (n). With R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K), it solves any single-condition gas problem. At **STP** (273 K, 1 atm), 1 mole of gas fills 22.4 L.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'kelvin': 'Using °C in a gas law is the #1 mistake. Convert to K first, every time.', 'inversely related': 'Inverse: one goes up, the other goes down. Squeeze a balloon (less volume) and the pressure goes up.' },
    recall: [['What does temperature measure in a gas?', 'The average kinetic energy of the particles.'], ['A gas at 2 L is heated from 300 K to 450 K at constant pressure. New volume?', '2 × 450/300 = 3 L (Charles\'s law).'], ['Convert 27 °C to kelvin.', '300 K.']],
    recap: ['KMT: fast, random particles; temperature = average KE; pressure = collisions.', 'Boyle (inverse P-V), Charles (direct V-T), Gay-Lussac (direct P-T).', 'Always use kelvin.', 'PV = nRT; 1 mol at STP = 22.4 L.'],
  },
  'chem/u7': {
    why: 'Most chemistry happens in water. Concentration, acids and bases explain your stomach, swimming pools and batteries - and they show up in nearly every lab.',
    guide: ['How is concentration measured?', 'What makes something an acid or a base?', 'What does the pH scale really mean?'],
    s: [
      ['Solutions and molarity', 'A **solution** is a **solute** dissolved in a **solvent**. **Molarity (M)** = moles of solute ÷ liters of solution. To dilute, use **M₁V₁ = M₂V₂**: the moles stay the same, only the volume changes.'],
      ['Acids, bases and pH', '**Acids** produce H⁺ (or H₃O⁺) in water; **bases** produce OH⁻. **Strong** acids like HCl ionize completely; **weak** acids like vinegar only partly ionize. When an acid and base react, they **neutralize** into a salt and water.', '**pH = −log[H⁺]**. Below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, above 7 is basic. Because it\'s a log scale, each step is **10 times**: pH 3 is 100× more acidic than pH 5. A **titration** adds a base of known concentration to an acid until neutralization, which lets you find the acid\'s concentration.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'M₁V₁ = M₂V₂': 'Adding water doesn\'t change how many moles you have - only how spread out they are.', '10 times': 'The log scale trips people up: a difference of 1 in pH is 10×, a difference of 2 is 100×.' },
    recall: [['0.5 mol in 0.25 L - molarity?', '2.0 M.'], ['What do acids and bases make when they neutralize?', 'A salt and water.'], ['How much more acidic is pH 2 than pH 5?', '10³ = 1000 times.']],
    recap: ['Molarity = mol ÷ L; dilution M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.', 'Acids give H⁺, bases give OH⁻; strong = fully ionized.', 'pH = −log[H⁺]; each unit is 10×.', 'Neutralization → salt + water; titration finds concentration.'],
  },
  'chem/u8': {
    why: 'Why do some reactions release heat while others need it? Why do some happen instantly and others take years? This unit answers **how much energy** and **how fast** - and what happens when a reaction runs both ways.',
    guide: ['What is the difference between endothermic and exothermic?', 'What speeds a reaction up?', 'How does a system at equilibrium respond to a change?'],
    s: [
      ['Energy in reactions', '**Exothermic** reactions release heat (ΔH < 0) - burning fuel. **Endothermic** reactions absorb heat (ΔH > 0) - cold packs. To calculate heat, use **q = mcΔT**: mass × specific heat × temperature change. Water\'s specific heat is 4.18 J/(g·°C), which is why oceans change temperature slowly.'],
      ['Rates', 'Particles must collide with enough energy - the **activation energy** - to react. Reactions go faster with higher **temperature**, higher **concentration**, more **surface area**, or a **catalyst**. A catalyst lowers the activation energy and isn\'t used up.'],
      ['Equilibrium', 'Some reactions are reversible. At **equilibrium**, the forward and reverse rates are equal, so concentrations stop changing - but the reactions don\'t stop. **Le Châtelier\'s principle**: if you disturb a system at equilibrium, it shifts to counteract the change. Add reactant → shifts toward products. Add heat to an exothermic reaction → shifts toward reactants.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'activation energy': 'Picture a hill the reactants must climb. A catalyst digs a tunnel through it.', 'Le Châtelier': 'Treat heat like a reactant or product in the equation. Then just push away from whatever you add.' },
    recall: [['Is a cold pack endothermic or exothermic?', 'Endothermic - it absorbs heat from your skin.'], ['Name four ways to speed up a reaction.', 'Raise temperature, raise concentration, increase surface area, add a catalyst.'], ['N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ + heat. Remove NH₃ - which way does it shift?', 'Toward products (right), to replace it.']],
    recap: ['Exothermic releases heat (ΔH < 0); endothermic absorbs it.', 'q = mcΔT.', 'Rate: temperature, concentration, surface area, catalyst (lowers Ea).', 'Equilibrium = equal rates; Le Châtelier: shift away from the stress.'],
  },
  // ---------------- Advanced Business Management ----------------
  'abm/u1': {
    why: 'Every business question you\'ll face - in class, DECA, or an internship - starts with "what is the manager actually doing?" The four functions of management are the framework for all of it.',
    guide: ['What are the four functions of management?', 'How do leadership styles differ, and when does each work?', 'What do managers at different levels focus on?'],
    s: [
      ['The four functions (POLC)', '**Planning** sets goals and decides how to reach them. **Organizing** arranges people and resources - job roles, departments, the org chart. **Leading** motivates and directs people. **Controlling** measures results against the plan and fixes gaps.', 'These form a loop: plan, organize, lead, then control - and what you learn while controlling feeds the next plan. Good goals are **SMART**: Specific, Measurable, Attainable, Relevant, Time-bound.'],
      ['Leadership styles and levels', '**Autocratic** leaders decide alone - fast, which helps in a crisis, but bad for morale over time. **Democratic (participative)** leaders involve the team, building buy-in. **Laissez-faire** leaders step back, which works with experts and fails with beginners. **Situational leadership** means matching the style to the team\'s experience.', '**Top managers** do strategic, long-range planning for the whole company. **Middle managers** turn strategy into tactical plans for departments. **First-line supervisors** run daily operations.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'Controlling': '"Compare results to a goal, then correct" = controlling. Students often confuse it with planning.', 'Situational leadership': 'For DECA: say "I\'d adjust my style to the team\'s experience" - judges like seeing situational thinking.' },
    recall: [['A manager compares monthly sales to the target and retrains staff. Which function?', 'Controlling.'], ['When does an autocratic style work best?', 'In emergencies or with inexperienced teams that need fast, clear direction.'], ['What level of manager writes the five-year strategy?', 'Top management.']],
    recap: ['POLC: plan, organize, lead, control (a loop).', 'SMART goals.', 'Styles: autocratic, democratic, laissez-faire; situational = match the team.', 'Levels: top (strategic), middle (tactical), first-line (operational).'],
  },
  'abm/u2': {
    why: 'Strategy is how a business decides where to compete and how to win. These tools - SWOT, Porter\'s Five Forces, competitive advantage - show up in every case competition.',
    guide: ['How do you sort SWOT items correctly?', 'What are the two ways to win against competitors?', 'What do Porter\'s Five Forces measure?'],
    s: [
      ['Mission, vision and SWOT', 'The **mission** says what the business does now and for whom. The **vision** says where it\'s going. A **SWOT analysis** sorts the situation into four boxes: **Strengths** and **Weaknesses** are **internal** (things you control), while **Opportunities** and **Threats** are **external** (the market, competitors, the economy).', 'The skill is deciding internal or external first, then good or bad. A new competitor is an external threat; outdated equipment is an internal weakness.'],
      ['Competitive advantage', 'A business wins through **cost leadership** (being the cheapest producer, like Walmart) or **differentiation** (being different or better in ways customers pay for, like Apple). Trying to do both at once without a plan is called being "stuck in the middle."', '**Porter\'s Five Forces** measure how attractive an industry is: rivalry among competitors, the threat of new entrants, the threat of substitutes, the bargaining power of buyers, and the bargaining power of suppliers. The stronger the forces, the harder it is to earn profits.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'internal': 'Ask "could the company fix this by itself?" If yes, it\'s internal (S/W).', 'substitutes': 'A substitute is a different product that meets the same need: video calls are a substitute for business travel.' },
    recall: [['A loyal customer base - which SWOT box?', 'Strength (internal, good).'], ['Name the two generic strategies.', 'Cost leadership and differentiation.'], ['List the Five Forces.', 'Rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power, supplier power.']],
    recap: ['Mission = now; vision = future.', 'SWOT: S/W internal, O/T external.', 'Win by cost leadership or differentiation.', 'Five Forces measure industry attractiveness.'],
  },
  'abm/u3': {
    why: 'Businesses run on people. Motivation theories show up as scenario questions: an employee says X - what is missing, and what should the manager do?',
    guide: ['What is the HR cycle?', 'How do Maslow and Herzberg explain motivation?', 'What is the difference between Theory X and Theory Y?'],
    s: [
      ['The HR cycle', 'Human resources follows a cycle: **plan** staffing needs → **recruit** applicants → **select** the best → **train** → **evaluate** performance → **compensate** (pay and benefits). **Job enrichment** adds responsibility to make a job more meaningful; **job rotation** moves people between tasks to build skills and reduce boredom.'],
      ['Motivation theories', '**Maslow\'s hierarchy**: people satisfy needs in order - physiological, safety, social (belonging), esteem, self-actualization. A worker worried about job security (safety) won\'t be motivated by recognition yet.', '**Herzberg\'s two-factor theory**: **hygiene factors** (pay, conditions, policies) prevent dissatisfaction but don\'t motivate. **Motivators** (achievement, recognition, growth, meaningful work) actually drive effort. **McGregor**: **Theory X** managers assume people avoid work and need control; **Theory Y** managers assume people want responsibility and motivate themselves.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'hygiene factors': 'Raising pay stops complaints but rarely creates excitement. That\'s Herzberg in one sentence.', 'Theory Y': 'Y = "yes, they want to work." X = "they need watching."' },
    recall: [['An employee is well paid but bored. Herzberg says what\'s missing?', 'Motivators: achievement, recognition, growth, meaningful work (job enrichment).'], ['What comes right before esteem in Maslow\'s hierarchy?', 'Social / belonging needs.'], ['A manager installs time clocks and close supervision. X or Y?', 'Theory X.']],
    recap: ['HR cycle: plan, recruit, select, train, evaluate, compensate.', 'Maslow: needs in order up the pyramid.', 'Herzberg: hygiene prevents dissatisfaction; motivators drive effort.', 'McGregor: Theory X controls; Theory Y trusts.'],
  },
  'abm/u4': {
    why: 'Operations is where the product actually gets made and delivered. Efficiency and quality decide whether a good idea makes money.',
    guide: ['How is productivity measured?', 'What are the trade-offs of just-in-time inventory?', 'How do companies manage quality and projects?'],
    s: [
      ['Productivity and inventory', '**Productivity** = output ÷ input, for example units per labor hour. The **supply chain** is every step from raw materials to the customer. **Just-in-time (JIT)** inventory has materials arrive right when they\'re needed. That cuts storage costs, but a single late shipment can stop production.'],
      ['Quality and projects', '**Quality control (QC)** inspects products for defects after they\'re made. **Quality assurance (QA)** designs processes to prevent defects in the first place. **Total Quality Management (TQM)** makes everyone responsible for quality, with **kaizen** - small, continuous improvements.', 'Projects are scheduled with **Gantt charts** (bars on a timeline). The **critical path** is the longest chain of dependent tasks: delay anything on it, and the whole project slips.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'Just-in-time': 'COVID-era shortages are a real example of JIT risk: one missing part stalled car factories for months.', 'critical path': 'Tasks off the critical path have "slack" - they can slip a little without delaying the finish date.' },
    recall: [['600 units in 20 labor hours - productivity?', '30 units per labor hour.'], ['QC vs QA?', 'QC detects defects after production; QA prevents them by designing better processes.'], ['Why does the critical path matter?', 'Any delay on it delays the entire project.']],
    recap: ['Productivity = output ÷ input.', 'JIT: low storage cost, high supply risk.', 'QC inspects; QA prevents; TQM + kaizen improve continuously.', 'Gantt charts and the critical path manage projects.'],
  },
  'abm/u5': {
    why: 'Managers decide how to reach customers. The 4 Ps and the product life cycle tell you which marketing move fits the moment.',
    guide: ['What are the 4 Ps?', 'How does strategy change across the product life cycle?', 'Which pricing strategy fits which situation?'],
    s: [
      ['The marketing mix', 'The **4 Ps** are **Product** (what you sell), **Price**, **Place** (distribution - where and how customers buy), and **Promotion** (advertising, social media, sales). **Segmentation** splits the market into groups: **demographic** (age, income), **geographic** (location), **psychographic** (values, lifestyle), and **behavioral** (how they use or buy). You choose a **target market** and tailor the 4 Ps to it.'],
      ['Life cycle and pricing', 'The **product life cycle**: **introduction** (build awareness), **growth** (sales rise, competitors arrive), **maturity** (sales peak, so defend share and differentiate), and **decline** (cut costs, refresh, or retire the product).', 'Pricing strategies: **skimming** starts high for early adopters, then lowers (new gaming consoles). **Penetration** starts low to grab share quickly (a new streaming service). **Cost-plus** adds a markup to cost. **Competitive** pricing matches rivals.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'psychographic': 'Psycho = mind: attitudes, values, interests. "Eco-conscious shoppers" is psychographic.', 'skimming': 'Skimming works when buyers are eager and have few substitutes. Penetration works when customers are price-sensitive.' },
    recall: [['Which P is "sold through Target and online"?', 'Place.'], ['Sales have peaked and copycats appear. Stage and move?', 'Maturity - differentiate, promote, defend share.'], ['Low launch price to win users fast is called?', 'Penetration pricing.']],
    recap: ['4 Ps: product, price, place, promotion.', 'Segmentation: demographic, geographic, psychographic, behavioral.', 'Life cycle: intro, growth, maturity, decline.', 'Pricing: skimming, penetration, cost-plus, competitive.'],
  },
  'abm/u6': {
    why: 'Managers make money decisions and ethical decisions every day. Break-even analysis and stakeholder thinking are both favorite case-competition tools.',
    guide: ['How do you calculate break-even?', 'Who are a company\'s stakeholders?', 'What is corporate social responsibility?'],
    s: [
      ['Budgets and break-even', 'An **operating budget** plans day-to-day revenue and expenses. A **capital budget** plans big long-term purchases like buildings and equipment. **Break-even** is where total revenue equals total cost. **Contribution margin** = price − variable cost per unit, and **break-even units = fixed costs ÷ contribution margin**.'],
      ['Stakeholders and ethics', '**Stakeholders** are everyone affected by the business: owners and shareholders, employees, customers, suppliers, the community, and government. **Shareholders** are only the owners. Good managers weigh all stakeholders, not just profit.', '**Corporate social responsibility (CSR)** means acting responsibly beyond the legal minimum - environment, community, fair labor. In ethics cases, a strong answer names the issue, lists who is affected, compares options, and recommends one with reasons.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'Contribution margin': 'Each sale first pays its own variable cost; what\'s left "contributes" toward fixed costs. Once those are covered, the rest is profit.', 'Stakeholders': 'Stakeholder > shareholder. Every shareholder is a stakeholder, but not every stakeholder is a shareholder.' },
    recall: [['Fixed costs $6,000, price $25, variable cost $10. Break-even units?', '6000 ÷ 15 = 400 units.'], ['Name four stakeholders of a factory.', 'Employees, customers, owners, the local community (also suppliers, government).'], ['What should a strong ethics answer include?', 'The issue, the stakeholders, the options, and a recommendation with reasons.']],
    recap: ['Operating vs capital budgets.', 'Break-even = fixed ÷ (price − variable).', 'Stakeholders: everyone affected; shareholders = owners.', 'CSR and a 4-step ethics frame.'],
  },
  // ---------------- Spanish 2 ----------------
  'span2/u1': {
    why: 'The present tense is the foundation for everything in Spanish 2. If you can conjugate stem-changers and yo-go verbs automatically, every other tense gets easier.',
    guide: ['How do regular present-tense endings work?', 'What is a "boot" verb?', 'Which verbs are irregular only in the yo form?'],
    s: [
      ['Regular endings', 'Drop -ar, -er or -ir and add endings for the person. -ar: **o, as, a, amos, an** (hablo, hablas, habla, hablamos, hablan). -er: **o, es, e, emos, en**. -ir: **o, es, e, imos, en**. The ending alone tells you who is doing the action, so Spanish often drops the subject pronoun.'],
      ['Stem-changers ("boot verbs")', 'Some verbs change the vowel in their stem in every form **except nosotros** (and vosotros). If you draw the conjugation chart and outline the forms that change, it looks like a **boot**. Three patterns: **e → ie** (querer → quiero), **o → ue** (poder → puedo, dormir → duermo), **e → i** (pedir → pido).', 'Nosotros stays regular: podemos, queremos, pedimos.'],
      ['Yo-go verbs', 'Some common verbs are irregular only in the yo form, which ends in **-go**: tengo (tener), hago (hacer), pongo (poner), salgo (salir), digo (decir), vengo (venir). Tener and venir are also stem-changers in the other forms: tienes, viene.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'except nosotros': 'Nosotros sits outside the boot. When in doubt, nosotros keeps the infinitive\'s stem.', '-go': 'Say them as a chant: tengo, hago, pongo, salgo, digo, vengo.' },
    recall: [['Conjugate poder for yo and nosotros.', 'Puedo, podemos.'], ['Which form never changes its stem in a boot verb?', 'Nosotros (and vosotros).'], ['Yo form of salir?', 'Salgo.']],
    recap: ['-ar: o as a amos an; -er: o es e emos en; -ir: o es e imos en.', 'Boot verbs: e→ie, o→ue, e→i; nosotros is outside the boot.', 'Yo-go: tengo, hago, pongo, salgo, digo, vengo.'],
  },
  'span2/u2': {
    why: 'Ser and estar both mean "to be," and mixing them up changes meaning (es aburrido = he\'s boring; está aburrido = he\'s bored). Reflexive verbs let you talk about your daily routine.',
    guide: ['When do you use ser vs estar?', 'What makes a verb reflexive?', 'Where does the reflexive pronoun go?'],
    s: [
      ['Ser vs estar', 'Use **ser** for **DOCTOR**: **D**escription, **O**ccupation, **C**haracteristic, **T**ime, **O**rigin, **R**elationship. "Es doctora," "Son las tres," "Somos de Texas."', 'Use **estar** for **PLACE**: **P**osition, **L**ocation, **A**ction (with -ando/-iendo), **C**ondition, **E**motion. "Estoy cansado," "La sopa está fría," "Estamos en la biblioteca." One trap: the location of an **event** uses ser - "La fiesta es en mi casa."'],
      ['Reflexive verbs', 'A **reflexive verb** means the subject does the action to itself: **levantarse** (to get up), **ducharse**, **acostarse**. The pronoun matches the subject: **me, te, se, nos, se**. "Me levanto a las seis."', 'The pronoun goes **before a conjugated verb** (me ducho) or **attached to an infinitive** (voy a ducharme).'],
    ],
    notes: { 'uses ser': 'Events "take place," so they use ser. People and things "are located," so they use estar.', 'attached to an infinitive': 'Both "me voy a duchar" and "voy a ducharme" are correct - just never put it in the middle.' },
    recall: [['"Mi padre ___ ingeniero" - ser or estar?', 'Es (occupation → ser).'], ['"Nosotros ___ en clase" - ser or estar?', 'Estamos (location → estar).'], ['Say "I go to bed at ten."', 'Me acuesto a las diez.']],
    recap: ['Ser = DOCTOR; estar = PLACE.', 'Event location uses ser.', 'Reflexive pronouns: me, te, se, nos, se.', 'Pronoun before the conjugated verb or attached to the infinitive.'],
  },
  'span2/u3': {
    why: 'The preterite lets you tell stories about what happened. It\'s the most-used past tense for completed events.',
    guide: ['What are the regular preterite endings?', 'Which spelling changes happen in the yo form?', 'Which common verbs are irregular?'],
    s: [
      ['Regular preterite', 'Use the preterite for **completed** actions at a specific time: ayer, anoche, el año pasado. -ar endings: **é, aste, ó, amos, aron** (hablé, hablaste, habló). -er and -ir share endings: **í, iste, ió, imos, ieron** (comí, viviste, salió).', 'The accents matter: hablo (I speak, present) and habló (he spoke, preterite) are different words.'],
      ['Spelling changes and irregulars', 'In the **yo** form, **-car → qué**, **-gar → gué**, **-zar → cé**: busqué, llegué, empecé. These keep the original sound.', 'Common irregulars take special stems with endings **e, iste, o, imos, ieron** and **no accents**: tener → tuv-, estar → estuv-, hacer → hic- (hizo), poder → pud-, querer → quis-, decir → dij- (dijeron). **Ser and ir share the same forms**: fui, fuiste, fue, fuimos, fueron - context tells you which.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'qué': 'Llegé would be pronounced "yeh-HEH," so the spelling changes to llegué to keep the hard g.', 'share the same forms': '"Fui al cine" = I went (ir). "Fui estudiante" = I was (ser). Context decides.' },
    recall: [['Yo form of buscar in the preterite?', 'Busqué.'], ['Él form of hacer in the preterite?', 'Hizo.'], ['How do you tell "fue" (ser) from "fue" (ir)?', 'Context: "fue a la tienda" (went) vs "fue difícil" (was).']],
    recap: ['Preterite = completed, specific time.', '-ar: é aste ó amos aron; -er/-ir: í iste ió imos ieron.', 'Yo spelling changes: -qué, -gué, -cé.', 'Irregular stems (tuv-, hic-, dij-) with no accents; ser = ir.'],
  },
  'span2/u4': {
    why: 'Real stories mix two past tenses. The imperfect paints the scene; the preterite moves the action. Choosing correctly is the biggest jump from Spanish 1 to 2.',
    guide: ['How is the imperfect formed?', 'When do you use the imperfect vs the preterite?', 'What trigger words help you choose?'],
    s: [
      ['Forming the imperfect', 'The imperfect is the most regular tense. -ar: **aba, abas, aba, ábamos, aban**. -er/-ir: **ía, ías, ía, íamos, ían**. Only three irregulars: **ser** (era), **ir** (iba), **ver** (veía).'],
      ['Choosing the tense', 'Think of a movie. The **imperfect** is the background: what was happening, habits, descriptions, time, age, weather, feelings. The **preterite** is the action: what happened, interruptions, a sequence of completed events.', '"**Leía** cuando **sonó** el teléfono" - I was reading (background) when the phone rang (interruption). Trigger words for imperfect: siempre, todos los días, mientras, cuando era niño. For preterite: ayer, de repente, una vez, el lunes pasado.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'Only three irregulars': 'Ser, ir, ver - that\'s the whole list. Everything else in the imperfect is regular.', 'background': 'Ask: "Is this the scenery or the plot?" Scenery = imperfect. Plot = preterite.' },
    recall: [['Imperfect of ir for nosotros?', 'Íbamos.'], ['"Cuando ___ (tener) diez años..." - which tense?', 'Imperfect: tenía (age in the past).'], ['"De repente, ___ (llover)" - which tense?', 'Preterite: llovió ("de repente" = sudden event).']],
    recap: ['Imperfect: aba / ía endings; irregulars ser, ir, ver.', 'Imperfect = background, habits, description, time, age.', 'Preterite = completed actions, interruptions, sequences.', 'Trigger words help you choose.'],
  },
  'span2/u5': {
    why: 'Commands and object pronouns make your Spanish sound natural - giving directions, ordering food, or saying "give it to me" instead of repeating nouns.',
    guide: ['How do affirmative and negative tú commands differ?', 'What are the irregular tú commands?', 'Where do object pronouns go?'],
    s: [
      ['Tú commands', '**Affirmative** tú commands look like the él/ella present form: habla, come, escribe. **Negative** tú commands use **no + the subjunctive**: no hables, no comas, no escribas.', 'Eight irregular affirmative tú commands: **di** (decir), **haz** (hacer), **ve** (ir), **pon** (poner), **sal** (salir), **sé** (ser), **ten** (tener), **ven** (venir). Memory trick: "Vin Diesel Has Ten Weapons" (ven, di, sal, haz, ten, ve, pon, sé).'],
      ['Object pronouns', '**Direct object** pronouns replace what receives the action: lo, la, los, las. **Indirect object** pronouns say to or for whom: me, te, le, nos, les. When you use both, **indirect comes first**, and **le/les becomes se** before lo/la: "Se lo doy" (I give it to her).', 'Pronouns **attach** to affirmative commands (¡Dímelo!) and go **before** negative commands (¡No me lo digas!). Add an accent when attaching keeps the original stress.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'no + the subjunctive': 'Negative commands flip the vowel: -ar verbs get -es (no hables), -er/-ir verbs get -as (no comas).', 'before lo/la': '"Le lo" never appears in Spanish - it always becomes "se lo."' },
    recall: [['Affirmative tú command of tener?', 'Ten.'], ['Negative tú command of comer?', 'No comas.'], ['Replace both objects: "Doy el libro a María."', 'Se lo doy.']],
    recap: ['Affirmative tú = él form; negative tú = no + subjunctive.', 'Irregulars: di, haz, ve, pon, sal, sé, ten, ven.', 'Indirect before direct; le/les → se.', 'Attach to affirmative, place before negative.'],
  },
  'span2/u6': {
    why: 'Grammar is the skeleton; vocabulary is the muscle. These themes - travel, health, childhood, environment - are the core Spanish 2 topics you\'ll read, write and talk about.',
    guide: ['How do you learn vocabulary so it sticks?', 'What are the key words for each theme?', 'How do you use new words in real sentences?'],
    s: [
      ['Learning words that stick', 'Learn words in **chunks**, not alone: "hacer la maleta" (to pack a suitcase) instead of just "maleta." Always learn nouns **with their article** (el viaje, la salud) so you know the gender. Say them out loud, use them in a sentence about your own life, and let spaced review bring back the ones you miss.'],
      ['Theme vocabulary', '**Travel**: el viaje, el aeropuerto, la maleta, el pasaporte. **Health**: la salud, el médico, la fiebre, el tobillo. **Childhood**: la niñez, el juguete (use the imperfect: "De niño, jugaba con..."). **Environment**: la naturaleza, el medio ambiente, reciclar, la contaminación.', '**Money and work**: el dinero, ahorrar (to save), gastar (to spend), el trabajo, la entrevista. **Media**: la película, la noticia, el periódico.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'chunks': 'Your brain stores phrases better than single words. A chunk gives you grammar for free.', 'imperfect': 'Childhood vocab pairs naturally with the imperfect - practice both together.' },
    recall: [['Why learn nouns with their article?', 'So you know the gender (el/la) for agreement.'], ['Say "I save money."', 'Ahorro dinero.'], ['Which tense pairs with talking about childhood?', 'The imperfect.']],
    recap: ['Learn in chunks, with articles, out loud, with spaced review.', 'Travel, health, childhood, environment, money, media themes.', 'Pair childhood vocab with the imperfect.'],
  },
  // ---------------- Math 3 ----------------
  'math3/u1': {
    why: 'Functions are the language of all higher math. Once you see transformations as simple moves - shift, stretch, flip - you can sketch almost any graph from its equation.',
    guide: ['What are domain and range?', 'How do inside and outside changes move a graph?', 'How do you find an inverse?'],
    s: [
      ['Functions, domain, range', 'A **function** gives exactly one output for each input. The **domain** is all allowed inputs; the **range** is all possible outputs. Two common domain limits: you can\'t take the **square root of a negative** (√(x − 4) needs x ≥ 4) and you can\'t **divide by zero**.'],
      ['Transformations', 'Start from a **parent function** (x², √x, |x|, 2ˣ). Changes **outside** the function act **vertically and as written**: f(x) + 3 moves up 3; 2f(x) stretches vertically. Changes **inside** act **horizontally and backwards**: f(x − 3) moves **right** 3; f(2x) compresses horizontally. A negative outside (−f(x)) flips over the x-axis; a negative inside (f(−x)) flips over the y-axis.', 'So (x + 3)² − 4 is x² moved left 3 and down 4, with its vertex at (−3, −4).'],
      ['Inverses', 'An **inverse** undoes a function. To find it, **swap x and y**, then solve for y. For f(x) = 2x + 6: x = 2y + 6 → y = (x − 6)/2. The graphs of a function and its inverse are reflections over the line **y = x**.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'backwards': 'Inside changes feel backwards because you\'re changing the input: x − 3 means you need a bigger x to get the same output, so the graph moves right.', 'swap x and y': 'Undo in reverse order: the function multiplied by 2 and then added 6, so the inverse subtracts 6 first, then divides by 2.' },
    recall: [['Domain of 1/(x − 5)?', 'All reals except x = 5.'], ['Describe f(x) = (x − 2)² + 1 from x².', 'Right 2, up 1; vertex (2, 1).'], ['Inverse of f(x) = 3x − 9?', 'f⁻¹(x) = (x + 9)/3.']],
    recap: ['Domain: watch square roots and division by zero.', 'Outside changes: vertical, as written. Inside: horizontal, backwards.', 'Negatives flip; multipliers stretch.', 'Inverse: swap x and y, solve; reflection over y = x.'],
  },
  'math3/u2': {
    why: 'Polynomials model profit, motion and design. Factoring and the quadratic formula are the tools for finding where they cross zero - their **roots**.',
    guide: ['How do you factor quadratics quickly?', 'When do you use the quadratic formula?', 'What does the leading term tell you about the graph?'],
    s: [
      ['Factoring and roots', 'Factoring rewrites a polynomial as a product. For x² + bx + c, find two numbers that **multiply to c and add to b**. Then use the **zero product property**: if (x − 3)(x + 5) = 0, then x = 3 or x = −5. Always **pull out a GCF first**, and watch for patterns like the difference of squares, a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b).'],
      ['The quadratic formula', 'When factoring is hard, the **quadratic formula** always works: x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a. The **discriminant**, b² − 4ac, previews the answer: positive means 2 real roots, zero means 1, and negative means 2 complex roots (no x-intercepts).'],
      ['Polynomial graphs', '**End behavior** comes from the leading term. Even degree with a positive leading coefficient: both ends point up. Odd degree with a positive leading coefficient: down on the left, up on the right. A root that appears twice (a double root) touches the x-axis and bounces off instead of crossing.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'multiply to c and add to b': 'This is exactly the "Find the pair" drill in Learn → Factoring. Practice it until it\'s automatic.', 'discriminant': 'Compute the discriminant first. It tells you whether to bother looking for real roots.' },
    recall: [['Solve x² − x − 12 = 0.', '(x − 4)(x + 3) = 0 → x = 4 or −3.'], ['Discriminant of x² + 2x + 5 - and what it means?', '4 − 20 = −16 < 0: no real roots.'], ['End behavior of −x³?', 'Up on the left, down on the right.']],
    recap: ['Factor: GCF first, then pair numbers or special patterns.', 'Zero product property gives the roots.', 'Quadratic formula always works; discriminant previews roots.', 'Leading term decides end behavior; double roots bounce.'],
  },
  'math3/u3': {
    why: 'Exponentials describe growth that speeds up - compound interest, populations, viral spread. Logs let you solve for time: "how long until my money doubles?"',
    guide: ['What are the exponent rules?', 'How do growth and decay models work?', 'What is a logarithm, really?'],
    s: [
      ['Exponent rules', 'Same base, multiplying: **add exponents** (x³ · x⁴ = x⁷). Dividing: **subtract** (x⁷ / x² = x⁵). Power of a power: **multiply** ((x³)² = x⁶). x⁰ = 1, and x⁻² = 1/x².'],
      ['Growth and decay', 'Exponential growth: **y = a(1 + r)ᵗ**, where a is the start amount and r is the growth rate. Decay: **y = a(1 − r)ᵗ**. A population of 500 growing 10% a year is 500(1.1)ᵗ. Unlike linear growth (adding the same amount), exponential growth multiplies by the same factor, so it speeds up over time.'],
      ['Logarithms', 'A **logarithm** asks: **what power?** log₂(8) = 3 because 2³ = 8. Logs undo exponents, so when the unknown is in the exponent, take a log of both sides. Properties: log(ab) = log a + log b; log(a/b) = log a − log b; log(aⁿ) = n·log a. Change of base: log_b(x) = log x / log b.'],
    ],
    notes: { '(1 + r)': 'The "1 +" keeps the original amount. Growing 10% means you keep 100% and add 10%, so you multiply by 1.10.', 'what power?': 'Read log₃(81) aloud as "3 to what power is 81?" The answer is 4.' },
    recall: [['Simplify x⁵ · x³ / x².', 'x⁶.'], ['$1,000 grows 5% a year. Expression after t years?', '1000(1.05)ᵗ.'], ['log₅(125) = ?', '3.']],
    recap: ['Exponent rules: add, subtract, multiply exponents.', 'Growth a(1 + r)ᵗ; decay a(1 − r)ᵗ.', 'A log is an exponent: log_b(x) = "b to what power is x".', 'Log properties turn products into sums.'],
  },
  'math3/u4': {
    why: 'Systems model real decisions with two or more conditions: two phone plans, a budget and a time limit. Solving them finds the point where both are true.',
    guide: ['What does the solution of a system mean on a graph?', 'When is elimination faster than substitution?', 'How do systems of inequalities work?'],
    s: [
      ['Solving systems', 'The solution to a system is where the graphs **intersect** - the point that makes both equations true. **Substitution**: solve one equation for a variable and plug it into the other. **Elimination**: add or subtract the equations, after scaling if needed, so one variable cancels.', 'Use elimination when the coefficients already line up (x + y = 10 and x − y = 2: add them to get 2x = 12). Always **check your answer in both equations**.', 'Parallel lines never meet, so there is **no solution**. The same line twice gives **infinitely many** solutions.'],
      ['Inequalities and applications', 'For systems of **inequalities**, graph each one and shade; the solution is the **overlap**. Use a dashed line for < or > and a solid line for ≤ or ≥. Word problems usually give you two "totals," such as total items and total cost. Each total becomes one equation.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'check your answer in both equations': 'On multiple-choice tests, plugging the choices in is often faster than solving.', 'two "totals': 'Example: 20 tickets totaling $230, with adult tickets $15 and student tickets $10: x + y = 20 and 15x + 10y = 230.' },
    recall: [['Solve x + y = 7, x − y = 1.', 'x = 4, y = 3.'], ['What does "no solution" look like on a graph?', 'Two parallel lines.'], ['Set up: 12 coins, quarters and dimes, worth $2.10.', 'q + d = 12; 0.25q + 0.10d = 2.10.']],
    recap: ['Solution = intersection point.', 'Substitution or elimination; check in both equations.', 'Parallel = no solution; same line = infinite solutions.', 'Inequality systems: shade, and the solution is the overlap.'],
  },
  'math3/u5': {
    why: 'Trigonometry measures what you can\'t reach directly - heights, distances, angles. The unit circle extends it to waves, sound, and anything that repeats.',
    guide: ['What do sine, cosine and tangent measure?', 'How do radians work?', 'What is the equation of a circle?'],
    s: [
      ['Right-triangle trig', 'From an angle, label the sides: **opposite**, **adjacent**, **hypotenuse**. Then **SOH-CAH-TOA**: sin = opp/hyp, cos = adj/hyp, tan = opp/adj. If the hypotenuse is 10 and the angle is 30°, the opposite side is 10 × sin 30° = 5.'],
      ['The unit circle and radians', 'On the **unit circle** (radius 1), each angle\'s point is **(cos θ, sin θ)**. Key values: 30° → (√3/2, 1/2), 45° → (√2/2, √2/2), 60° → (1/2, √3/2). **Radians** measure angles by arc length: **π radians = 180°**, so 90° = π/2.', 'Signs by quadrant: **All** positive in quadrant I, **Sine** in II, **Tangent** in III, **Cosine** in IV - "All Students Take Calculus."'],
      ['Circles', 'A circle with center (h, k) and radius r: **(x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²**. The signs inside flip, just like transformations: (x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 9 has center (2, −1) and radius 3.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'SOH-CAH-TOA': 'Always label opposite/adjacent from the angle you\'re using - they swap if you switch angles.', 'π radians = 180°': 'To convert degrees to radians, multiply by π/180. For radians to degrees, multiply by 180/π.' },
    recall: [['Opposite 3, hypotenuse 5. sin θ?', '3/5.'], ['Convert 60° to radians.', 'π/3.'], ['Center and radius of (x + 4)² + y² = 16?', 'Center (−4, 0), radius 4.']],
    recap: ['SOH-CAH-TOA from the chosen angle.', 'Unit circle point = (cos θ, sin θ); memorize 30/45/60.', 'π rad = 180°.', 'Circle: (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r².'],
  },
  'math3/u6': {
    why: 'Statistics turns data into decisions - test scores, polls, investment returns. Probability tells you how likely an outcome is before it happens.',
    guide: ['How do mean, median and standard deviation describe data?', 'What is the 68-95-99.7 rule?', 'How do you combine probabilities?'],
    s: [
      ['Describing data', 'The **mean** is the average; the **median** is the middle value. Outliers pull the mean but barely move the median, so use the median for skewed data like incomes. **Standard deviation** measures spread: a small SD means the data cluster near the mean.'],
      ['The normal distribution', 'Many measurements form a bell-shaped **normal distribution**. The **68-95-99.7 rule**: about 68% of the data is within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2, and 99.7% within 3. A **z-score**, z = (x − mean) / SD, tells you how many SDs a value is from the mean.'],
      ['Probability', 'P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). For **independent** events (one doesn\'t affect the other), P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Two coin flips both landing heads: ½ × ½ = ¼. **Conditional probability** P(A | B) is the probability of A given that B happened.'],
    ],
    notes: { 'median': 'Real-world rule: report the median for income and house prices, because a few billionaires distort the mean.', 'z-score': 'z = 2 means "2 standard deviations above average" - better than about 97.5% of values in a normal distribution.' },
    recall: [['Mean 70, SD 5. What % scored 60-80?', '~95% (within 2 SD).'], ['z-score of 85 if mean 75, SD 5?', '2.'], ['P(rolling a 6 twice)?', '1/36.']],
    recap: ['Mean vs median: the median resists outliers.', 'SD measures spread.', 'Normal distribution: 68-95-99.7; z = (x − μ)/σ.', 'Independent events: multiply; "or": add then subtract the overlap.'],
  },
};
